artial? The mandate also includes period of each use, identification of natural persons, etc. Proposal focuses on reference DB linking. It satisfies requirement for identifying reference databases while... It may not cover all logging events. But effectiveness: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability? Maybe 3 contributes to solution. Let's interpret: the stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Immutable versioning addresses a key part (reference DB) but not necessarily all logging capabilities like start/end time, input data for match, identification of persons. So it's partial.\n\nThus effectiveness = 3? Or 4? It says satisfies requirement for identifying reference databases while significantly improving internal reproducibility. The mandate includes more than that. Might be partial solution. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Could argue 4 if you think logging mandate is just reference DB? No, Article 12 includes many. So 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nContent-addressable storage + automatic metadata tagging for every production request, cryptographically link model versions to reference DB. That's significant engineering change, requires infrastructure overhaul. Not trivial. Likely significant change, expensive/difficult. So 2? Or 3? Some changes reasonably feasible? Might be significant. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\nThis is internal technical system for compliance and reproducibility. Acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Could be 3 somewhat uncommon? Content-addressable storage is not radical. I'd say 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving\n\nProposal introduces new elements (immutable versioning) but still treats compliance as logging practice? It says improves internal reproducibility metrics. Might be weaving logging into science? Cryptographically link model versions to reference DB prevents data drift obscuring decision. That's more than just compliance logging; it changes relationship between R&D and compliance? Could be paradigm stretching: logging woven into science. Might be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common mundane boring\n\nImmutable dataset versioning system with content-addressable storage for AI compliance is perhaps interesting but not extremely novel; versioning datasets is known. Maybe 2 = interesting? Or 3 unusual? I'd say 2 = interesting (not rare ingenious). Could be 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. Hmm.\n\nThe reference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many labs might propose logging infrastructure, dataset versioning is somewhat common. Might be 2.\n\nLet's choose originality 2.\n\nCheck order: applicability evidence then applicability score; effectiveness evidence then effectiveness score; implementability evidence... etc.\n\nNeed output format? Probably JSON with fields. In