oduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: proposal adds a classification board and documentation updates but keeps R&D and compliance separate, treating compliance as an overlay to logging rather than redefining research.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3= unusual interesting; shows some imagination 2=interesting 1=common mundane boring\n\nThis is common governance audit. Likely common, mundane. Originality 1.\n\nEvidence: early risk classification and quarterly compliance reviews are routine practices expected from experienced evaluators for AI Act conformity.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\"... first and then \"...\". Could output list.\n\nI'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2927\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: The protocol directly targets identifying which Article 12 logging mandates apply and aligning development with them.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Early classification informs necessary instrumentation and documentation updates, which helps compliance but does not itself create the automatic logging systems required by Article 12.\neffectiveness: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: A formal governance board for regulatory classification is a socially and legally normal compliance practice in regulated R&D labs.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Setting up a quarterly review board requires process changes and staff time but can be done with existing personnel and documentation systems.\nimplementability: 3\n\nparadig